WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.437
Time series pre-training promises to cut labeling
costs and boost many downstream tasks,

00:00:06.517 --> 00:00:11.222
but the standard recipes borrowed from vision and
language fall short.

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:17.239
Randomly masking a series or computing series-wise
similarity tends to distort or ignore

00:00:17.319 --> 00:00:21.273
the temporal correlations that make time series
meaningful.

00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:23.392
This paper introduces TimeSiam,

00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:28.520
a simple but effective self-supervised framework
built on Siamese networks.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.708
Instead of masking in isolation,

00:00:30.788 --> 00:00:36.792
TimeSiam samples a past and a current subseries
from the same sequence and trains Siamese

00:00:36.872 --> 00:00:41.442
encoders to reconstruct the masked current series
from the past one,

00:00:41.522 --> 00:00:45.475
explicitly modeling how observations correlate
across time.

00:00:45.555 --> 00:00:52.449
Learnable lineage embeddings let a single model
capture correlations at many different time distances.

00:00:52.529 --> 00:00:58.192
Across thirteen benchmarks and two mainstream
tasks, forecasting and classification,

00:00:58.272 --> 00:01:03.730
TimeSiam sets a new state of the art in both
in-domain and cross-domain settings.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.231
Self-supervised pre-training has transformed vision
and language,

00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:13.595
and researchers naturally reached for the same
tools when working with time series.

00:01:13.675 --> 00:01:15.081
But the fit is awkward.

00:01:15.161 --> 00:01:18.377
When you randomly mask points across a time series,

00:01:18.457 --> 00:01:23.095
you can shatter the smooth temporal structure that
the signal depends on,

00:01:23.175 --> 00:01:27.231
sometimes making reconstruction so hard the model
learns little.

00:01:27.311 --> 00:01:33.048
Contrastive learning has the opposite problem:
comparing whole series for similarity tends

00:01:33.128 --> 00:01:36.409
to ignore the fine-grained correlations inside them,

00:01:36.489 --> 00:01:42.225
and it hinges on augmentations that are notoriously
difficult to design for temporal data.

00:01:42.305 --> 00:01:47.783
The gap this paper addresses is clear: existing
recipes fail to emphasize the temporal

00:01:47.863 --> 00:01:50.950
correlations that make time series what they are.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:55.879
The timing could not be better for a method that
gets this right.

00:01:55.959 --> 00:02:00.508
Every day the world's sensors, wearables,

00:02:00.588 --> 00:02:05.137
and industrial systems pour out staggering volumes
of unlabeled time series through the Internet of Things.

00:02:05.217 --> 00:02:09.766
That data is a gold mine, but only if we can learn
from it without hand labeling.

00:02:09.846 --> 00:02:14.394
The key insight motivating TimeSiam is that time
series carry a special kind of information

00:02:14.474 --> 00:02:19.023
prior methods throw away: the correlation between
what happened in the past and what is

00:02:19.103 --> 00:02:23.652
happening now.

00:02:23.732 --> 00:02:28.281
Instead of treating each window in isolation,

00:02:28.361 --> 00:02:32.910
why not build a pre-training task that explicitly
asks the model to relate distant moments in time to each other.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:37.194
This paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:37.274 --> 00:02:41.178
First, it proposes TimeSiam,

00:02:41.258 --> 00:02:45.162
a simple but effective pre-training framework that
uses Siamese networks to capture correlations

00:02:45.243 --> 00:02:49.147
among temporally distanced subseries.

00:02:49.227 --> 00:02:53.131
Second, it introduces learnable lineage embeddings,

00:02:53.211 --> 00:02:57.115
a lightweight mechanism that lets one model
represent many different past-to-current time distances.

00:02:57.195 --> 00:03:01.099
Third,

00:03:01.179 --> 00:03:05.083
through extensive experiments it shows TimeSiam
achieves consistent state-of-the-art results

00:03:05.163 --> 00:03:09.068
when fine-tuned on both forecasting and classification,

00:03:09.148 --> 00:03:13.052
and across both in-domain and cross-domain settings.

00:03:13.132 --> 00:03:17.036
Crucially, the framework is backbone-agnostic,

00:03:17.116 --> 00:03:21.020
dropping cleanly onto modern encoders like
iTransformer, PatchTST, and TCN.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.164
Here is how TimeSiam works.

00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:29.992
From a single time series it randomly samples two
windows: a past subseries and a current subseries,

00:03:30.072 --> 00:03:32.313
together called Siamese subseries.

00:03:32.393 --> 00:03:37.981
The current window is lightly corrupted with a
simple augmentation such as masking.

00:03:38.061 --> 00:03:42.897
Two shared-weight Siamese encoders process the past
and current windows,

00:03:42.977 --> 00:03:50.544
and a decoder with cross-attention reconstructs the
masked current series using the past observation as context.

00:03:50.624 --> 00:03:55.187
That single design choice, reconstructing the
current from the past,

00:03:55.267 --> 00:04:00.035
forces the model to internalize how distant moments
in a series relate.

00:04:00.115 --> 00:04:05.634
To handle the fact that past and current windows
can be near or far apart in time,

00:04:05.714 --> 00:04:10.892
TimeSiam adds learnable lineage embeddings indexed
by that relative distance,

00:04:10.972 --> 00:04:14.443
so one model fluently represents many temporal gaps.

00:04:14.523 --> 00:04:22.090
The training objective is simply the squared
reconstruction error between the true and predicted current series.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.193
The evaluation is deliberately broad,

00:04:26.273 --> 00:04:29.996
spanning thirteen benchmarks and two mainstream tasks.

00:04:30.076 --> 00:04:33.799
For forecasting there are the four ETT subsets,
plus Weather, Electricity, Traffic,

00:04:33.879 --> 00:04:37.602
and Exchange.

00:04:37.682 --> 00:04:41.405
For classification there are two EEG datasets, AD
and TDBrain, and an ECG dataset,

00:04:41.485 --> 00:04:45.208
PTB.

00:04:45.288 --> 00:04:49.012
On top of these eleven established benchmarks,

00:04:49.092 --> 00:04:52.815
the authors construct two new large-scale,

00:04:52.895 --> 00:04:56.618
multi-domain datasets called TSLD-500M and TSLD-1G.

00:04:56.698 --> 00:05:00.421
The larger one packs nearly fourteen million
examples drawn from diverse,

00:05:00.501 --> 00:05:04.224
non-overlapping domains,

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:08.027
which lets the paper stress-test cross-domain
transfer where pre-training and fine-tuning

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:11.830
data come from entirely different sources.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.806
The results are consistent and strong.

00:05:14.886 --> 00:05:16.567
On in-domain forecasting,

00:05:16.647 --> 00:05:23.117
TimeSiam cuts average mean squared error by five
point seven percent with a PatchTST backbone

00:05:23.197 --> 00:05:26.287
and two point five percent with iTransformer,

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:31.146
and remember these backbones already forecast very
well from scratch.

00:05:31.226 --> 00:05:33.118
On in-domain classification,

00:05:33.198 --> 00:05:38.894
it lifts average accuracy by eleven point five
percent over random initialization.

00:05:38.974 --> 00:05:44.528
Across all these settings TimeSiam beats eight
strong self-supervised baselines.

00:05:44.608 --> 00:05:50.797
Perhaps the most striking finding is in
cross-domain transfer: pre-training on the large,

00:05:50.877 --> 00:05:57.417
diverse TSLD-1G dataset and fine-tuning elsewhere
sometimes beats even in-domain pre-training,

00:05:57.497 --> 00:06:02.770
which confirms that scale and diversity of
pre-training data really pay off.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:08.329
Careful ablations on the Traffic benchmark tell us
which design choices matter.

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:14.321
Reconstructing the current window from a past one
clearly beats plain self-reconstruction,

00:06:14.401 --> 00:06:16.518
validating the core Siamese idea.

00:06:16.598 --> 00:06:22.576
The masking ratio has a sweet spot around
twenty-five percent: masking only fifteen percent

00:06:22.656 --> 00:06:25.838
makes the task too easy to teach anything useful,

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:29.366
while masking seventy-five percent makes it too hard.

00:06:29.446 --> 00:06:34.092
Lineage embeddings deliver consistent gains over
random initialization,

00:06:34.172 --> 00:06:40.017
and adding more of them keeps improving results on
Electricity and Traffic up to a point,

00:06:40.097 --> 00:06:45.210
confirming that explicitly modeling multiple
temporal distances is worthwhile.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:51.446
To put the impact in numbers: TimeSiam reduces
average forecasting error by five point

00:06:51.526 --> 00:06:56.209
seven percent on PatchTST and two point five
percent on iTransformer,

00:06:56.289 --> 00:07:02.352
and raises classification accuracy by eleven point
five percent in the in-domain setting,

00:07:02.432 --> 00:07:04.974
all relative to training from scratch.

00:07:05.054 --> 00:07:10.979
It does this across thirteen benchmarks covering
forecasting and classification in both

00:07:11.059 --> 00:07:13.464
in-domain and cross-domain settings,

00:07:13.544 --> 00:07:18.158
and it outperforms eight state-of-the-art
self-supervised baselines.

00:07:18.238 --> 00:07:21.057
Backing the cross-domain story is TSLD-1G,

00:07:21.137 --> 00:07:27.890
a newly built pre-training dataset with nearly
fourteen million examples spanning multiple domains.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:30.584
The lasting takeaway is a reframing.

00:07:30.664 --> 00:07:36.901
By posing time series pre-training as
past-to-current reconstruction between Siamese subseries,

00:07:36.981 --> 00:07:43.218
and equipping the model with learnable lineage
embeddings to span different temporal distances,

00:07:43.298 --> 00:07:49.802
TimeSiam captures exactly the correlations that
masking and contrastive methods leave on the table.

00:07:49.882 --> 00:07:54.789
The payoff is a simple, general framework that
drops onto modern backbones,

00:07:54.869 --> 00:07:57.515
scales with larger and more diverse data,

00:07:57.595 --> 00:08:02.170
and sets a new state of the art for transfer across
tasks and domains.
